<body>
<h1>Shotcut Shared Scripting and MCP API</h1>
<p class="notice"><strong>Proof of concept.</strong> This API is experimental and may change.
MCP tools and Coproducer Scripts mode are available. Local scripts are trusted code,
not sandboxed, and can access files and the network. No consent dialog is shown in this POC.</p>
<nav aria-label="Contents"><ul>
  <li><a href="#conventions">Conventions</a></li>
  <li><a href="#qml">QML usage</a></li>
  <li><a href="#mcp">MCP usage</a></li>
  <li><a href="#undo-and-revisions">Undo and revisions</a></li>
  <li><a href="#errors">Errors and limitations</a></li>
  <li><a href="#reference">API reference</a></li>
  <li><a href="#tools">MCP tool reference</a></li>
  <li><a href="#building">Building and contributing</a></li>
</ul></nav>

<h2 id="conventions">Conventions</h2>
<p>The same typed C++ API supplies QML methods, the capabilities catalog, MCP tools,
and this generated reference. Properties are read-only. Methods have three kinds:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Behavior</th><th>MCP access</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>read</td><td>Reads project information</td><td>Individual tool or a batch operation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>edit</td><td>Changes the project through undoable commands</td><td><code>shotcut_edit</code> batch</td></tr>
    <tr><td>action</td><td>Playback, playhead, or selection changes; not undoable</td><td>Individual tool or a batch operation</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Unmarked helper methods are QML-only: they are described in the catalog but are not
exported as MCP tools or accepted in edit batches. For example, QML has
<code>shotcut.frames()</code> and <code>shotcut.timecode()</code>;
MCP clients use the project frame rate to convert times.</p>
<p>Times are integer frames at <code>shotcut.project.profile.fps</code>, not seconds.
At 25 fps, one second is 25 frames. Clip <code>in</code> and <code>out</code> are
inclusive source frames; timeline <code>start</code> is the clip's start on its track.
Clip range filters use overlap with a half-open <code>[start, end)</code> interval.</p>
<p>For <code>timeline.removeClip()</code>, <code>moveClip()</code>, and
<code>trimClip()</code>, <code>ripple</code> defaults to false. When enabled, it
affects the edited track (or the source and destination tracks of a move), not
unrelated tracks or markers. GUI Ripple All Tracks and Ripple Markers settings
do not alter these API operations.</p>
<p>Tracks and playlist items use zero-based indexes. Track 0 is the top track.
Clip indexes include gaps and can change after edits. Use the returned UUID
<code>id</code> as <code>clipId</code> when editing clips; do not retain clip indexes
across edits. Marker indexes can change after removal. Re-read indexes and revisions
before a later edit. A split returns <code>{left, right}</code>;
the right half retains the original id.</p>
<p>Results are plain objects and arrays, not editable model objects.
Changing a returned object does not change Shotcut. Use edit methods instead.
The reference uses JSON type names: <code>integer</code>, <code>number</code>,
<code>boolean</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>array</code>, and <code>object</code>.
<code>any</code> denotes a dynamic result; read the method description for its shape.
An optional positional argument is shown with <code>?</code>.</p>

<h2 id="qml">QML usage</h2>
<p>Playlist edits use indexes into the complete source bin, not its current UI filter.
<code>playlist.addItem({resource, index?, in?, out?})</code> imports a local media file
or MLT virtual clip; the default is to append. <code>removeItem(index)</code>,
<code>moveItem(index, toIndex)</code>, and <code>trimItem(index, {in?, out?})</code>
remove, reorder, and set inclusive source ranges. These edits do not alter existing
timeline clips or delete media files. Re-read indexes after removal or reordering.
Import follows Shotcut's image-duration and proxy settings; background proxy jobs
are not canceled by undo or batch rollback.</p>
<p><code>timeline.appendClip(options)</code>, <code>insertClip(options)</code>, and
<code>overwriteClip(options)</code> place new independent clips on an explicit
<code>trackIndex</code>. Give exactly one <code>resource</code> (local file) or
<code>playlistIndex</code> (complete source-bin index), and optional inclusive
<code>in</code>/<code>out</code>. A playlist source is copied with its filters and
does not change when the timeline copy is edited. Append uses the track end; insert
and overwrite require a nonnegative <code>position</code> in timeline frames.
Insert shifts later footage; overwrite replaces only the covered range without
shifting later footage. Positions beyond the track end create a gap.</p>
<p>Insert offers explicit <code>rippleAllTracks</code> and <code>rippleMarkers</code>
options, both defaulting to false, independent of the timeline toolbar settings.
Ripple All Tracks affects only unlocked tracks. Ripple Markers shifts markers
starting at or after the insertion point, including their range ends.
Append and overwrite leave other tracks and markers unchanged.
These methods clear timeline clip selection but do not select the new clip or seek.
Insertion that splits transitions or joined clips, and overwrite covering transitions
or joined clips, are not supported yet.</p>
<h3>Clip selectors</h3>
<p><code>timeline.clipIds({trackIndex?, start?, end?, selectedOnly?})</code>
returns ordinary clip IDs in track/timeline order. No options means all clips;
<code>{selectedOnly: true}</code> means the current clip selection. Combine options
to intersect criteria, for example selected clips on one track overlapping a range.
Ranges are timeline frames and half-open: a clip matches <code>[start, end)</code>
when it ends after start and starts before end. Boundaries must be nonnegative
integers, and end must exceed start when both are given.</p>
<p>Selectors never change selection or history, and do not exclude locked tracks:
edit methods enforce locks. Blanks and transitions are always excluded from
<code>clipIds()</code>. To read full snapshots with the same criteria, use
<code>timeline.clips()</code>; it also accepts <code>includeBlanks</code> (default false)
and <code>includeTransitions</code> (default true, preserving existing behavior).
Resolve track names through <code>timeline.tracks()</code>; names need not be unique.</p>
<pre><code>const ids = shotcut.timeline.clipIds({
    trackIndex: 0, selectedOnly: true, start: 100, end: 250
})
shotcut.undo.macro("Brightness on matching clips", function () {
    for (const id of ids)
        shotcut.filters.add({clipId: id}, "brightness", {level: 1.2})
})</code></pre>
<p>MCP exposes <code>timeline_clip_ids</code> with the same named criteria.
The result is an array of strings, not clip objects. It can also be used as a read
operation inside <code>shotcut_edit</code>, with aliases such as
<code>$matches.0</code>. The batch does not automatically loop over results;
clients construct the edit operations for the returned IDs.</p>
<h3>Filters</h3>
<p><code>filters.available()</code> lists installed Shotcut metadata IDs and target
restrictions; <code>filters.metadata(metadataId)</code> returns the native MLT metadata
under <code>mlt</code>, including parameter descriptions, types, ranges, and declared
defaults. Native fields are strings exactly as declared, without UI conversions;
missing fields remain absent. <code>metadataAvailable</code> indicates whether MLT
provided metadata. These declarations can be incomplete and are not necessarily
the defaults set by Shotcut's filter UI.</p>
<p>Filter targets are exactly one of <code>{clipId: id}</code>,
<code>{trackIndex: 0}</code>, or <code>{output: true}</code>.
<code>filters.list(target)</code> returns regular user filters and their parameters.
Use <code>add(target, metadataId, parameters?)</code>,
<code>remove(target, index)</code>, <code>move(target, index, toIndex)</code>,
<code>setEnabled(target, index, enabled)</code>, and
<code>setParameters(target, index, parameters)</code> for undoable edits.
Creation accepts only installed Shotcut metadata IDs, not arbitrary MLT services,
and uses native MLT defaults plus explicit parameters without executing filter QML.
There is no automatic conversion from UI percentages or sliders.
Selecting a filter later can apply additional behavior from its normal QML UI.</p>
<p>Values may be finite numbers, booleans, strings, or native rectangles
<code>{x, y, width, height, opacity}</code>. Declared MLT types and readonly flags
are checked where supplied. Undeclared native properties are permitted because
metadata is not exhaustive. Internal identity, timing, and enable properties are
protected. Keyframe strings and modifications to animated parameters are not yet
supported. Edits leave the target selection and playhead alone; removing the
currently displayed filter clears its UI and may select a remaining filter. Timeline filter indicators
update for affected clips and tracks even when they are not selected.
Filter indexes are panel rows
(including user links), not raw MLT indexes. Re-read after add/remove/move.
Clip filter ranges include footage used by adjacent transitions, just as in the
Filters panel. Track and output filters follow their normal duration-pinning behavior.
API structural edits use normal Filters panel selection behavior for the displayed
target, including selecting added filters. A selected list row corresponds to the
filter displayed in the parameter UI.
Links, filter sets, blanks, transitions, locked tracks, and changes to built-in
track volume filters are excluded from these writes.</p>
<pre><code>const target = {clipId: shotcut.timeline.selection()[0].id}
const meta = shotcut.filters.metadata("brightness")
console.log(JSON.stringify(meta.mlt.parameters))
const added = shotcut.filters.add(target, "brightness", {level: 1.2})
shotcut.filters.setParameters(target, added.index, {level: 1.1})
shotcut.filters.setEnabled(target, added.index, false)</code></pre>
<p>Select <strong>Scripts</strong> in Coproducer. Missing reference examples are copied from
the installation's <code>share/shotcut/scripts</code> directory into the user scripts
folder without overwriting existing files. Use <strong>Open Folder</strong> to locate it.
Each top-level <code>*.qml</code> file appears in the picker by filename; no manifest is needed.
Discovery does not execute scripts. Entering Scripts mode shows
<strong>Select a script to run</strong> until you choose one from the picker.
Only the selected file is loaded, with
<code>shotcut</code> available as a context property. Leaving Scripts mode unloads it.</p>
<p>Use an <code>Item</code> or <code>Rectangle</code> as the root. Inline JavaScript and QML
components can live in the same file. Relative helper imports are supported, but helper QML
files in the discovery root also appear in the picker. <strong>Reload</strong> unloads the
panel, clears its engine's component cache, and rescans files. Loading and runtime warnings
appear below the panel. Reload keeps the selection if the file still exists.
Deleting an example restores it on the next activation or reload.</p>
<p>Reference scripts are maintained in <code>src/qml/scripts</code>, with developer
comments explaining API usage, undo, and job handling. <code>ProjectDashboard.qml</code> displays live project and selection
information. <code>ClipNavigator.qml</code> filters clip names case-insensitively and
matches durations at least as long as the minimum seconds. It can select all matches,
clear selection, or select and seek to an individual clip. Its actions do not edit
the project or create undo entries.</p>
<p><code>AddTransitions.qml</code> pairs consecutive selected clips on each track and
adds dissolve/audio crossfade transitions in one undo step. Choose a duration in frames
and either overlap existing footage or preserve the combined clip duration using spare
source frames before the right clip's in point. Close Gaps includes gap-separated pairs;
turn it off to skip them. Ripple closes vacated time on the affected track; Ripple All
Tracks also removes that time from other unlocked tracks. Markers stay in place.
Locked tracks, insufficient footage, and intervening clips or transitions are skipped
with reasons; other valid pairs are still applied.</p>
<p>QML calls methods with positional arguments. A trailing options map groups named
settings or filters. Omit it only when the method provides a default.</p>
<pre><code>// Read clips on the top track.
const clips = shotcut.timeline.clips({trackIndex: 0})

// Set the first clip's fade-out to one second.
if (clips.length &gt; 0)
    shotcut.timeline.updateClip(clips[0].id, {
        fadeOut: Math.round(shotcut.project.profile.fps)
    })

// Playback actions do not add undo entries.
shotcut.timeline.seek(250)
shotcut.player.play({speed: 1})
shotcut.player.pause()</code></pre>
<p>Properties have change notifications where applicable, so QML property bindings
can update automatically. Snapshots returned by methods do not update themselves;
query again when the relevant state changes. Errors in an associated QML engine
are JavaScript exceptions with <code>message</code> and <code>code</code>:</p>
<pre><code>try {
    shotcut.timeline.setSelection(["invalid-id"])
} catch (error) {
    console.error(error.code + ": " + error.message)
}</code></pre>
<p>Use <code>shotcut.undo.macro(text, callback)</code> for a named synchronous edit
group; see <a href="#undo-and-revisions">Undo and revisions</a> for rollback rules and examples.</p>

<h2 id="mcp">MCP usage</h2>
<p>The in-app MCP server uses the standard MCP <code>tools/list</code> and
<code>tools/call</code> protocol. Its listener is local to Shotcut; use the address
reported by the running application. The examples show the <code>params</code>
portion of a <code>tools/call</code> request.</p>
<p>Start with <code>shotcut_state</code> for properties and summaries.
Use <code>shotcut_capabilities</code> with an object or method path for discovery.
Read and action tool names convert camelCase to snake_case:
<code>timeline.setSelection</code> becomes <code>timeline_set_selection</code>.</p>
<pre><code>{"name": "shotcut_state", "arguments": {}}
{"name": "shotcut_capabilities", "arguments": {"path": "timeline.splitClip"}}
{"name": "timeline_clips", "arguments": {"trackIndex": 0}}
{"name": "timeline_seek", "arguments": {"position": 250}}</code></pre>
<p>MCP uses named arguments. Options are normally flattened alongside positional
parameter names. A nested <code>options</code> object is also accepted by the
invoker, but generated tool schemas expose the flattened form.</p>
<p>Use <code>shotcut_edit</code> to group changes into one undo step.
Each operation has an API <code>path</code>, named <code>args</code>, and an optional
<code>as</code> alias for its result. Here <code>CLIP_UUID</code> must be replaced
with a clip id, frame 100 must be inside that clip, and <code>expectedRevision</code>
must match the revision returned by your preceding read:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "name": "shotcut_edit",
  "arguments": {
    "label": "Split and fade",
    "expectedRevision": 12,
    "ops": [
      {
        "path": "timeline.splitClip",
        "args": {"clipId": "CLIP_UUID", "position": 100},
        "as": "split"
      },
      {
        "path": "timeline.updateClip",
        "args": {"clipId": "$split.left.id", "fadeOut": 25}
      },
      {
        "path": "timeline.setSelection",
        "args": {"clipIds": ["$split.left.id"]}
      }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Aliases can reference a whole result (<code>$split</code>), a field
(<code>$split.left.id</code>), or an array element (<code>$clips.0.id</code>).
References are resolved recursively inside argument objects and arrays.
Use <code>$$</code> to escape a literal leading dollar sign.
Aliases must be unique, must not contain dots or begin with a dollar sign, and
may only refer to earlier operations. Operations execute in order.
A client can loop over clips, assemble up to 500 operations, and submit one
batch without consuming one undo entry per clip.</p>
<p>Tool results are available as MCP <code>structuredContent</code> (and a JSON text
content block). A read or standalone action returns:</p>
<pre><code>{"status": "ok", "revision": 12, "result": ...}</code></pre>
<p>A batch returns per-operation results. <code>undoText</code> is present only
when undoable edits were committed:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "status": "ok",
  "revision": 13,
  "undoText": "Coproducer: Split and fade",
  "results": [{"path": "timeline.splitClip", "result": ...}]
}</code></pre>
<p>The downloads <a href="api.json">api.json</a> and
<a href="mcp-tools.json">mcp-tools.json</a> contain the generated API catalog and
actual shared MCP tool descriptions and input schemas. Auxiliary tools such as
forum and documentation search are outside this reference.</p>

<h2 id="undo-and-revisions">Undo and revisions</h2>
<h3>Background jobs and exports</h3>
<p><code>jobs.list()</code> and <code>jobs.job(id)</code> read the normal Jobs queue.
Job IDs are stable until the job is removed or Shotcut exits. Status is one of
<code>pending</code>, <code>starting</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>paused</code>,
<code>canceling</code>, <code>canceled</code>, <code>succeeded</code>, or <code>failed</code>.
Snapshots include progress (0-100), elapsedMs, and canCancel. Failed jobs include
an error message; <code>jobs.log(id, maxCharacters?)</code> reads a bounded diagnostic
log tail. A queued export is not a completed export: poll its job IDs.</p>
<p><code>jobs.cancel(id)</code> cancels pending or active work without removing the job
or deleting partial output files. Active cancellation is asynchronous. Completed jobs
are unchanged. Cancel and export launch are not undoable, cannot run inside
<code>shotcut_edit</code> or <code>undo.macro()</code>, and do not change project revision.</p>
<p><code>jobs.exportPresets()</code> lists stock and custom presets with stable IDs:
<code>default</code>, <code>stock:consumer/avformat/x264-medium</code> (the full MLT
registry key), and <code>custom:filename</code> (the actual preset filename).
<code>jobs.startExport(target, presetId, {overwrite?})</code> exports a snapshot of
the full timeline, returning <code>{jobIds, jobs, target}</code>. Use an absolute local
filename in an existing writable directory. Existing output is rejected unless overwrite
is true; project media, the project file, and outputs of active jobs cannot be replaced.
Preset fields are passed directly to the MLT consumer; the Export controls' conversions,
format-dependent normalization, processing-mode adjustments, and saved hardware encoder
or decoder preferences are not applied. The consumer may supply defaults for fields absent
from a preset. The API's <code>default</code> preset explicitly selects MP4, software
libx264, AAC, CRF 23, fast x264 preset, and fast-start; dimensions and frame rate come from
the project profile unless a profile-specific preset supplies them. Hardware encoders are
used only when explicitly stored in the selected preset. Hardware decoding is always
disabled for API exports. Subtitle feeds are included.
<code>meta.preset.*</code> and underscore-prefixed fields are treated as preset metadata,
not consumer options; the extension field is only a hint and does not change the target
filename. <code>v2pass=true</code> or <code>pass=1</code>/<code>pass=2</code> requests
the normal two-pass pair, with each pass property generated for its job. Invalid or
conflicting pass markers fail instead of being silently ignored.</p>
<p>Streaming, source/bin exports, range exports, image sequences, arbitrary encoder
properties, and filter analysis launch are not included. Missing media or filters
still requiring analysis cause an explicit error, not a dialog or automatic analysis.
Jobs follow existing queue pause/scheduling behavior.</p>
<pre><code>const result = shotcut.jobs.startExport("/absolute/path/output.mp4", "default")
const state = shotcut.jobs.job(result.jobIds[0])
// Later: shotcut.jobs.cancel(result.jobIds[0])</code></pre>
<p>MCP tools are <code>jobs_list</code>, <code>jobs_job</code>, <code>jobs_log</code>,
<code>jobs_cancel</code>, <code>jobs_export_presets</code>, and <code>jobs_start_export</code>.
The <code>JobsAndExport.qml</code> example provides preset/output selection, live job
progress, cancellation, and log viewing.</p>
<p>Synchronous QML edits from one script callback are grouped into one undo entry
prefixed with <code>Script: </code>. A successful MCP edit batch creates one undo entry prefixed with
<code>Coproducer: </code>. Failed batches roll back undoable changes.
Empty, read-only, and action-only batches create no undo entry.
The transaction foundation also groups synchronous QML edit calls into an
automatic macro that closes after the script returns to the event loop.</p>
<p>While a script or MCP edit transaction is open, a non-cancelable application-modal
dialog prevents GUI interaction with the project. Nested groups share this dialog,
which remains active until commit or rollback finishes. Command-owned progress and
error dialogs can still appear above it. The dialog does not cover AI/network waits,
standalone reads or actions, or background jobs.</p>
<p>Use <code>shotcut.undo.macro(text, callback)</code> for an explicit, named,
synchronous group. It returns the callback's result. Any thrown value or API error
rolls back the group, even if an API error is caught inside the callback.
Nested macros share the outer group and its label. If an automatic group is already
open, the explicit macro joins it; failure rolls back that entire group when it closes.</p>
<pre><code>try {
    const count = shotcut.undo.macro("Batch Brightness", function () {
        const clips = shotcut.timeline.selection()
        for (const clip of clips)
            shotcut.filters.add({clipId: clip.id}, "brightness", {level: 1.2})
        return clips.length
    })
} catch (error) {
    console.warn("Edits rolled back: " + error)
}</code></pre>
<p>The installed <code>BatchBrightness.qml</code> reference script demonstrates adding
or updating Brightness on selected clips with one named undo entry.
<code>shotcut.undo.running</code> is true during a callback; the Scripts panel disables
mode selection, script selection, and Reload while it runs. The helper always closes
its scope on return or error, so it does not leave an open macro on script reload.</p>
<p><strong>Callbacks must be synchronous.</strong> Do not use async functions, Promises,
timers, network requests, or other deferred edits inside the callback. Prepare asynchronous
results first, then apply them in a synchronous macro. Async functions and Promise
return values are rejected; already scheduled external work cannot be canceled.
Manual start/stop macros remain deferred. This QML-only helper is not an MCP tool;
MCP clients use <code>shotcut_edit</code> for equivalent grouping and rollback.</p>
<p><strong>Actions are never rolled back or undone.</strong> A seek, playback change,
or selection applied earlier in a failing batch remains applied.
Selection changes caused by ordinary editing commands may also be visible.
Put actions at the end of a batch when that matches the intended workflow.</p>
<p>Project <code>revision</code> is a change token, not a persistent project id or
a frame counter. A committed transaction advances it once; failed transactions
and standalone actions do not advance it. Other project changes and undo/redo
can also advance it. Supply <code>expectedRevision</code> to prevent a stale batch
from running. A foreign open transaction returns <code>busy</code>.</p>
<p class="notice">Known POC limitations: opening an undo macro discards existing redo
history even if the macro is empty or later rolled back. Automatic track creation
after a move empties a track may produce a separate undo entry.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors and limitations</h2>
<p>Do not treat null or an empty list alone as an MCP error: some reads legitimately
return no match. Check <code>status</code>. Errors contain a stable code and a
human-readable message. A batch may also include a zero-based <code>opIndex</code>
and <code>rolledBack: true</code> when execution failed and rollback ran.
Prevalidation errors do not require rollback.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "status": "error",
  "code": "not_found",
  "message": "Clip ... is not in the timeline.",
  "revision": 12,
  "opIndex": 1,
  "rolledBack": true
}</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>invalid_argument</td><td>Missing, unknown, malformed, or out-of-range argument</td></tr>
    <tr><td>not_found</td><td>Unknown API path, clip, track, marker, or playlist index</td></tr>
    <tr><td>unavailable</td><td>Required media/timeline missing, track locked, or operation unsupported in the current state</td></tr>
    <tr><td>busy</td><td>Another transaction owns the edit context</td></tr>
    <tr><td>stale_revision</td><td>Read again before retrying the edit</td></tr>
    <tr><td>result_too_large</td><td>Read response exceeds the 100,000-byte limit; request fewer clips using available filters</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>There is no pagination or dry-run mode yet. Oversized successful edit batches
remain successful but omit their results and include a message instructing you
to read the results separately. Do not retry those edits as if they failed.</p>
<p>Timeline writes reject unsupported transition operations. Non-ripple moves
require empty destination space; lengthening requires available source media and
appropriate adjacent space. Filter keyframe editing and analysis remain deferred;
exports currently support the full timeline only.
<code>timeline.seek</code> fails if the timeline cannot be loaded into the
player, including when the existing GUI seek path has no producer to switch from.</p>
<p>Coproducer chat allows up to 15 tool rounds per prompt. This is separate from
the 500-operation batch limit. When the chat limit is reached, it shows a diagnostic
notice; earlier successful edits remain applied.</p>

<h2 id="reference">Generated API reference</h2>
%%API_REFERENCE%%

<h2 id="tools">Generated MCP tool reference</h2>
%%MCP_REFERENCE%%

<h2 id="building">Building and contributing</h2>
<p>The shared operations, catalog, invocation, and transactions live in <code>src/api</code>.
MCP transport and adapters, including tool generation and edit batches, live in
<code>src/mcp</code>. AI providers live in <code>src/coproducer</code>; the Coproducer
dock coordinates chat and scripts. The documentation generator intentionally uses
both the API catalog and MCP adapter to produce this shared reference.</p>
<p><code>docs/src/scripting-api.html.in</code> is the input template; generation
replaces its API and MCP reference placeholders and writes a complete
<code>index.html</code> alongside the JSON catalogs.</p>
<p>With <code>SHOTCUT_BUILD_TESTS=ON</code>, the <code>exportpresetproperties</code>
CTest covers preset IDs, resolution, profile extraction, and two-pass properties
without launching Shotcut or encoding video. It requires installed MLT preset and
profile data; set <code>MLT_DATA</code> if MLT cannot locate that data.</p>
<p>In Shotcut, choose <strong>Help &gt; API Documentation...</strong> to generate a fresh
reference in the application cache and open it in your default browser.
No source checkout, build tools, or network connection is required.</p>
<p>From a configured native build environment:</p>
<pre><code>cmake --build &lt;build-directory&gt; --target api-docs-check
cmake --build &lt;build-directory&gt; --target shared-api-docs</code></pre>
<p>The generated site is at <code>&lt;build-directory&gt;/docs/scripting-api/index.html</code>.
The default native build runs the completeness check. With <code>BUILD_DOCS=ON</code>,
the existing <code>docs</code> target also builds this site alongside the existing
QDoc reference for filter-development and internal QML APIs.
Cross-compiling does not run the target executable; generate and check these
docs from a native build.</p>
<p>The executable supports headless developer commands, which do not open Shotcut,
initialize MLT, load a project, or modify settings:</p>
<pre><code>shotcut --check-api-docs
shotcut --generate-api-docs &lt;HTML-template&gt; &lt;output-directory&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Add documentation next to API declarations using <code>Q_CLASSINFO</code>:</p>
<pre><code>Q_CLASSINFO("doc", "Object description.")
Q_CLASSINFO("doc.someProperty", "Property description.")
Q_CLASSINFO("mcp.someMethod", "read")
Q_CLASSINFO("doc.someMethod", "Behavior and returned data.")
Q_CLASSINFO("arg.someMethod.clipId", "Clip id.")
Q_CLASSINFO("arg.someMethod.count", "integer!|Required option.")
Q_CLASSINFO("arg.someMethod.enabled", "boolean|Optional setting.")</code></pre>
<p>The catalog infers positional types and requiredness from Qt meta-objects.
For a trailing <code>QVariantMap options</code>, metadata specifies option types
and requiredness (<code>!</code>). The checker requires descriptions for every
API object, property, method, positional parameter, and option, and types for
parameters and options. It also rejects duplicate paths and invalid method kinds.
No descriptions are copied into this template or a separate runtime table:
the generated API reference uses the same catalog as MCP discovery, and the
MCP reference uses the same tool definitions as the live server.</p>
</body>
</html>
